{% extends "pages/_example.jinja" %}
{% set example_slug = 'pipeline-tee' %}
{% set example_facts = [
  ('Level', 'Intermediate'),
  ('Needs', 'Python ' ~ python_floor ~ ' or later, and echo on PATH (any POSIX system)'),
  ('Install', 'Cuprum from main@' ~ cuprum_ref ~ '; see Getting started'),
  ('Tested', cuprum_verified_on ~ ' against main@' ~ cuprum_ref ~ ' on Linux, Python ' ~ python_floor ~ '; the failure run was repeated eight times with identical output'),
  ('Uses', 'Pipeline via |, RunOutputOptions(echo=True), PipelineResult.stages, failure_index'),
] %}
{% set pipeline_figure = {
  'id': 'fig-pipeline', 'num': 'Fig. 02', 'horizontal': true,
  'title': 'Which stream flows where, and what does the caller get back?',
  'steps': [
    {'kind': 'process', 'kind_label': 'Stage 0', 'name': 'echo -n "routed through two stages"', 'detail': 'stdout is piped to stage 1; nothing is captured here.', 'edge': 'bytes (stdout → stdin)'},
    {'kind': 'process', 'kind_label': 'Stage 1 (final)', 'name': 'python -c UPPER', 'detail': 'Its stdout is the pipeline’s output.', 'edge': 'bytes (final stdout)'},
    {'kind': 'observer', 'kind_label': 'Tee', 'name': 'echo=True', 'detail': 'Copies the bytes to the terminal as they arrive; capture keeps them too.', 'edge': 'captured stdout'},
    {'kind': 'result', 'name': 'PipelineResult', 'detail': 'stdout, ok, and one result per stage with its exit code: stages[], failure, failure_index.'},
  ],
  'branches': [
    {'when': 'A non-final stage exits non-zero while others still run', 'then': 'Fail-fast: Cuprum terminates every other still-running stage, upstream and downstream, and records the first failure.'},
    {'when': 'The final stage exits non-zero', 'then': 'No fail-fast termination is needed; the failure is still recorded as failure_index.'},
    {'when': 'Any stage writes to stderr', 'then': 'With echo=True every stage’s stderr is teed; only the final stage’s stdout is.'},
  ],
  'takeaway': 'Only the final stage’s stdout is captured and echoed. Intermediate stdout feeds the next stage and is reported as None.',
  'source': 'Source: cuprum/_pipeline_internals.py and docs/users-guide.md#pipeline-execution at main@' ~ cuprum_ref,
} %}

{% block problem %}
<p>
  A job needs two programs in a row — one produces text, the next transforms
  it — and you want to watch the result in the terminal while still keeping it
  for the code that follows. In a shell that is <code>a | b | tee</code> and a
  prayer about which stage failed. In Cuprum it is a <code>Pipeline</code>
  built with <code>|</code>, run once, returning a result per stage.
</p>
<p>
  The second stage here is the running Python interpreter itself, so the
  example needs nothing installed beyond <code>echo</code>. Because
  <code>sys.executable</code> is an absolute path, the catalogue records that
  exact path.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block code %}
{% call ui.code_panel('pipeline_tee.py', 'Complete', foot=[verified]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import (
    ECHO,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    RunOutputOptions,
    ScopeConfig,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(ECHO, PYTHON, name="pipeline-tee")

echo = sh.make(ECHO, catalogue=CATALOGUE)
python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)

UPPER = "import sys; sys.stdout.write(sys.stdin.read().upper())"

pipeline = echo("-n", "routed through two stages") | python("-c", UPPER)

with scoped(ScopeConfig(allowlist=CATALOGUE.allowlist)):
    # echo=True tees the final stdout and every stage's stderr to the
    # terminal while the final stdout is still captured.
    result = pipeline.run_sync(output=RunOutputOptions(echo=True))

print()
print(f"ok={result.ok}")
print(f"captured={result.stdout!r}")
for index, stage in enumerate(result.stages):
    print(index, stage.exit_code, repr(stage.stdout))
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
<div class="mt-10">
  {{ ui.flow(pipeline_figure) }}
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block output %}
<div class="cu-pair">
  {% call ui.output('Expected output', 'terminal') %}
ROUTED THROUGH TWO STAGES
ok=True
captured='ROUTED THROUGH TWO STAGES'
0 0 None
1 0 'ROUTED THROUGH TWO STAGES'
  {% endcall %}
  <div class="cu-prose">
    <p>
      The first line is the tee: the final stage's output, echoed as it
      arrived. The <code>print()</code> after the run starts a new line,
      because the output had none.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then the result: the pipeline succeeded, its captured
      <code>stdout</code> is the final stage's output, and each stage has its
      own exit code. Stage 0's <code>stdout</code> is <code>None</code>
      because its bytes went to stage 1, not to the caller.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block failure %}
<div class="cu-prose">
  <p>
    Now a three-stage pipeline where the middle stage gives up. The first
    stage writes forever; the second reads one line and exits with status 3;
    the third copies whatever it receives.
  </p>
</div>
<div class="cu-pair mt-8">
  {% call ui.code_panel('fail_fast.py', 'Failure path', foot=[verified], flat=true) %}
  {% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import sys

from cuprum import (
    ECHO,
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    ScopeConfig,
    scoped,
    sh,
)

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(ECHO, PYTHON, name="pipeline-tee")

python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)

# A producer that never stops, a filter that gives up, and a consumer.
PRODUCE = "import sys\nwhile True: sys.stdout.write('tick\\n'); sys.stdout.flush()"
REJECT = "import sys; sys.stdin.readline(); sys.exit(3)"
CONSUME = "import sys; sys.stdout.write(sys.stdin.read())"

pipeline = python("-c", PRODUCE) | python("-c", REJECT) | python("-c", CONSUME)

with scoped(ScopeConfig(allowlist=CATALOGUE.allowlist)):
    result = pipeline.run_sync()

print(f"ok={result.ok}")
print(f"failure_index={result.failure_index}")
print(f"failure exit={result.failure.exit_code if result.failure else None}")
for index, stage in enumerate(result.stages):
    print(index, stage.exit_code)
  {% endhighlight %}
  {% endcall %}
  {% call ui.output('Observed output', 'stdout, Linux') %}
ok=False
failure_index=1
failure exit=3
0 -15
1 3
2 -15
  {% endcall %}
</div>
<div class="cu-prose mt-10">
  <p>
    Stage 1 was the first non-final stage to exit non-zero while others were
    still running, so Cuprum terminated both of them. On POSIX a negative
    exit code names the signal that ended the process: <code>-15</code> is
    <code>SIGTERM</code>. Without fail-fast the producer would have written
    until something else stopped it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>failure_index</code> names the stage that failed first, and
    <code>failure</code> is its <code>CommandResult</code>. Later non-zero
    exits are usually consequences of the first, which is why only the first
    counts.
  </p>
  {% call ui.callout('note', 'Timing is part of the output') %}
  <p>
    The terminated stages' codes depend on what they were doing when the
    signal arrived. A stage that had already finished keeps its own exit
    code. In eight consecutive runs on Linux the output above did not vary,
    but treat the <code>-15</code> values as observed, not promised;
    <code>failure_index</code> and the failing stage's exit code are the
    contract.
  </p>
  {% endcall %}
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block policy %}
<p>
  The pipeline's catalogue lists two programs, <code>echo</code> and this
  interpreter's absolute path, and the scope's allowlist is that catalogue's.
  Cuprum checks every stage against the scope before any stage is spawned, so
  a pipeline with one forbidden stage runs none of them.
</p>
<p>
  Using <code>sys.executable</code> keeps the example self-contained. In real
  code, catalogue the tools the pipeline actually needs — the allowlist is the
  list a reviewer reads.
</p>
{% endblock %}
